I. "forbid committing" (your second message): possible only as a real wall,
   and there is exactly one: an agent must not own the files a commit writes.
   A hook or a `git` wrapper is one `--no-verify` or `/usr/bin/git` away and
   is not worth building. The workspace user has passwordless sudo inside the
   container (container/firstrun.sh installs with it), so any mount made
   inside the container is undone the same way -- a wall needs sudo gone
   first. Proposal, in order: (1) firstrun installs everything it will ever
   need during its root phase and drops the workspace user's sudo after it
   (heaptrack/valgrind/samply move into that phase); (2) `wk claude` asks
   podman (as root, from outside) to bind-mount `.git/{objects,refs,logs,HEAD,
   packed-refs}` read-only in the container's mount namespace for the session
   and unbinds them at exit -- so while an agent runs in a workspace, nobody in
   that workspace can commit, `git add`, stash, switch branches or rebase
   (`git status`, `diff`, `log`, builds and edits keep working); (3) `wk
   verify` measures both (no sudo; the five paths read-only) and `wk claude`
   refuses without them. A sibling agent container is not an option: the
   checkout is podman's own overlay (`:O,upperdir=`) and two mounts of one
   upperdir are unsafe; a separate agent uid is the other real wall but means
   re-owning every checkout. Say go, and whether the person's own in-workspace
   commits may be blocked for the session too (the proposal blocks them; the
   alternative is the uid split). Until then: an agent can commit. [decision]

